(102314,'https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Cookbook%3ASachertorte_(Duramecho_Version)','en','CC-BY-SA-3.0','Wikibooks — Cookbook:Sachertorte (Duramecho Version)','austrian','dessert',3,'The little bubbles in the egg white foam are what inflate the cake. Do not accidentally get any fat (from the egg yolks, butter, or chocolate) in the egg white mix or it will not foam properly.
Do not allow flour lumps in the mix, because lumps of white flour show up badly in this brown cake.
To get the traditional perfectly cylindrical shape, trim the cake body into a circle with vertical edges, and cut the top off flat before glazing.
Adding alcohol to the cake is not a good idea. It is already very moist, and more liquid will make it soggy.
Don''t use white chocolate in the cake body because it is fairly unnoticeable in flavour and colour, leaving only the moistening effect.
Don''t use self-raising flour, which creates large bubbles in the cake.
If carried out in the order above, no tool should need to be washed up more than once.
There are several other recipes for Sachertorte—some use even more eggs in the cake and egg and cream in the glaze.','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Sachertorte_DSC03027.JPG/960px-Sachertorte_DSC03027.JPG',640,480,'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sachertorte_DSC03027.JPG','David Monniaux','CC BY-SA 3.0',NULL,30,8,0,337.61,8.53,19.4,4.55,56.13,18.36,2.59,65.39,'B',67,0.875),
