(212336,'https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Cookbook%3ARoast_Turkey_with_Stuffing','en','CC-BY-SA-3.0','Wikibooks — Cookbook:Roast Turkey with Stuffing','american','side',3,'If you can''t find pre-made stock in a store then you''ll have to make it. You should know, however, turkey stock requires four hours of simmering. So make your stock the day before you make the turkey and stuffing. If you don''t have the time to make turkey stock then use pre-made chicken stock which all supermarkets carry. [http://www.foodnetwork.com/recipes/guy-fieri/turkey-stock-recipe/index.html Here''s] a great recipe for turkey stock from the Food Network.
Cooking turkey and stuffing involves the risk of salmonella poisoning. To prevent this, cook the turkey to 185 degrees Fahrenheit and the stuffing to 165 degrees Fahrenheit. Also, avoid cross contamination by keeping raw turkey away from all foods and wash your hands thoroughly after handling it.[http://www.fsis.usda.gov/Fact_Sheets/Salmonella_Questions_&_Answers/index.asp Health guidelines for preparing turkey and stuffing from the United States Department of Agriculture][http://www.hc-sc.gc.ca/hl-vs/iyh-vsv/food-aliment/turkey-dinde-eng.php Health guidelines for preparing turkey and stuffing from the Canadian government]
The most effective way to measure food temperatures is with a [http://www.consumersearch.com/digital-thermometers digital meat thermometer].','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/RoastTurkey.jpg/960px-RoastTurkey.jpg',640,425,'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RoastTurkey.jpg','Photo by M. Rehemtulla','CC BY 2.0',NULL,240,6,0,458.13,24.86,49.4,3.84,17.18,10.68,3.01,1391.14,'B',71,1),
