(38535,'https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Cookbook%3AHummus_(Greek)','en','CC-BY-SA-3.0','Wikibooks — Cookbook:Hummus (Greek)','greek','breakfast',3,'1–2 tablespoons of asafoetida (hing) powder can be used to substitute for the garlic, which may otherwise be omitted.
Use a pot that will hold several times the volume of the chickpeas and the water for cooking the chickpeas, because they will foam (the tablespoon of oil minimises that, however).
Use olive oil instead of the reserved cooking liquid to thin the hummus. Alternatively, mix in a tablespoon of olive oil into a serving before eating.
It''s particularly good when eaten with sliced French stick (bread), and it is traditionally eaten with [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pita pita] bread. It is also a nutritionally sound alternative to most other dips.
Hummus freezes really well, so consider making a double batch, and then freezing most of it in containers small enough to hold two servings (they can be defrosted overnight in the refrigerator, as needed).
Hummus is NOT a traditional Greek dish—it derives from Cyprus and the Middle East. Chickpeas in the traditional Greek cuisine (at least in Attica and Cyclades) are usually eaten only as soup.','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Hummus.jpg/960px-Hummus.jpg',640,440,'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hummus.jpg','Donovan Govan.','CC BY-SA 3.0',NULL,1440,NULL,1,558.16,23.4,28.66,9.96,59.38,1.16,4.66,1481.06,'B',69,1),
