(415033,'https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Cookbook%3AKubaneh_(Yemenite_Pull-Apart_Bread)','en','CC-BY-SA-3.0','Wikibooks — Cookbook:Kubaneh (Yemenite Pull-Apart Bread)','middle_eastern',NULL,3,'During the winter months, some had a custom to add to the baking pot along with the Kubaneh a piece of the fatty-tail of sheep,{{Citation|contribution=The Yemenite Cuisine|title=Ascending the Palm Tree: An Anthology of the Yemenite Jewish Heritage|last1=Mizrachi|first1=Avshalom|author-link1=|editor1=Rachel Yedid|editor2=Danny Bar-Maoz|publisher=E''ele BeTamar|place=Rehovot|page=134|year=2018|language=en|oclc=1041776317}} in which case the bread became a meat-dish. When the bread was eaten as a dairy dish, they would pour butter over the dough portions before inserting the pot in the oven.Kubaneh (Sabbath bread), {{YouTube|s8JgA4w-hhM|Kubaneh by Savta Shoshana}}, how to make Kubaneh (in Hebrew and in English) / May 2018','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Kubaneh_bread.jpg/960px-Kubaneh_bread.jpg',640,484,'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kubaneh_bread.jpg','Davidbena','CC BY-SA 4.0',NULL,180,8,0,256.57,6.69,4.3,0.52,48,0.01,0.04,317.13,'B',65,0.75),
