(92850,'https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Cookbook%3APoached_Egg','en','CC-BY-SA-3.0','Wikibooks — Cookbook:Poached Egg',NULL,NULL,3,'Fresher eggs keep their shape more easily than older eggs.
Use a heavy-based saucepan, since the heat distribution on its bottom is more even.
A tablespoon of vinegar can be added to the water, since this helps the eggs to set rapidly and prevents the egg white from billowing.
Agitating the water slightly by making a small whirlpool helps to prevent billowing as well as sticking
Leaving 10–15 seconds between adding eggs helps prevent them from sticking, particularly in small pans, as the earlier egg will have lightly set on the outside.
Proper cooking: A fundamental issue is how long eggs are cooked and at what temperature. These considerations are similar to boiling or coddling eggs, but because the egg is more spread out, it can cook more briefly.
Billowing: Poached eggs are ideally compact, rather than spread out or “billowed”, as these latter are delicate and messy. Gently adding the eggs to the water and using vinegar helps reduce billowing.
Yolk: Similarly to frying, the egg yolk should remain intact. Make sure to not pierce the yolk as you''re cracking the egg.','https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Rice-bowl-risotto-food-thumbnail%281%29.jpg',640,426,'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rice-bowl-risotto-food-thumbnail(1).jpg','AnonymousUnknown author','CC BY-SA 4.0',NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,0),
